<Guide lang="en" slug="building-on-your-own-land-barbecue-in-apartments" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="sleutelbeheer" ctaTitle="An apartment in a complex?" ctaText="We keep an eye on your apartment while you are away, and we know how the comunidad works.">
  <h2 id="short">Is it allowed?</h2>
  <p>That depends on your complex. Many apartment complexes on the Costa del Sol and in the Axarquía ban barbecues on terraces and balconies, especially charcoal ones. Elsewhere they are allowed, or only electric ones. No national law covers it: it is in the rules of the owners' community (<em>comunidad de propietarios</em>).</p>

  <h2 id="rules">Where the rules are</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Statutes</strong> (<em>estatutos</em>): part of the deed that divided the building, often recorded in the property register. Changing them in principle requires all owners.</li>
    <li><strong>House rules</strong> (<em>normas de régimen interior</em>): rules on day-to-day use, such as pool hours, pets and barbecues. The meeting can adopt or change these by majority.</li>
    <li><strong>The Horizontal Property Act</strong> (<em>Ley de Propiedad Horizontal</em>, art. 7.2): owners and occupants may not carry out activities that the statutes prohibit or that are a nuisance, unhealthy or dangerous to the neighbours. So even without an express ban, lots of smoke can be a problem.</li>
    <li><strong>Town hall</strong>: some towns have rules on smoke and fire in built-up areas. Extra restrictions can apply in the fire season.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Even a terrace that only you use is often legally common property with private use. You cannot simply build on it, for example a masonry barbecue.</p>

  <h2 id="community">What the community can do</h2>
  <p>If an owner or tenant does not follow the rules, the president first asks them in writing to stop. If that does not help, the meeting can decide to go to court (<em>acción de cesación</em>). The court can prohibit the use and award damages. It rarely comes to that, but in a complex you do not want a bad relationship with the neighbours.</p>

  <h2 id="check">How to find out</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Ask the property manager (<em>administrador de fincas</em>) or the president for the <strong>statutes</strong> and the <strong>house rules</strong>.</li>
    <li>Also ask for the <strong>minutes</strong> (<em>actas</em>) of recent meetings: sometimes a decision has been taken that is not yet in the rules.</li>
    <li>Looking at an apartment to buy? Have the agent or your lawyer request these documents before you buy. An outdoor kitchen or barbecue on the terrace is a reason for many buyers to choose an apartment.</li>
    <li>Want the rules changed? Put it on the agenda of the annual meeting. A shared barbecue area by the pool is often easier to arrange than a rule for every terrace.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="alternatives">What is often allowed</h2>
  <table class="prices plain">
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Good to know</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Electric table grill or plancha</td><td>No open flame and little smoke. Often allowed; check the rules.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Electric terrace barbecue</td><td>A real grill without charcoal. Mind the capacity of your electricity supply.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gas barbecue</td><td>Banned in some complexes because of the gas bottle. Check the rules and your insurance.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shared barbecue area of the comunidad</td><td>Needs a decision of the meeting; the costs are shared.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Have a house with its own garden? Read <a href="/en/guides/building-on-your-own-land-spain/outdoor-kitchen/">outdoor kitchen and barbecue</a>, including the fire-season rules.</p>
